  "meta": {
    "title": "Squid Beak Rush: The Chitin Gold Rush of the Gulf",
    "date": "2026-09-19",
    "lesson": "When a jumbo squid (Dosidicus gigas) dies and sinks off the Gulf of California, its chitinous beak and mantle hit the seafloor as a sudden, concentrated pulse of an otherwise scarce polymer. Copiotrophic bacteria such as Vibrio and Pseudoalteromonas that can barely persist in open water carry chitinases specifically to exploit these rare bonanzas: they sense the leaking N-acetylglucosamine breakdown products from meters away, race in, and bloom explosively for days. That very success is their undoing - dense, fast-dividing clonal patches are the perfect target for lysis, and bacteriophage populations tuned to the winning lineage crash the bloom within a week, releasing the leftover carbon for the next opportunist. This level asks you to win the chitin race with chemotaxis and a chitin-specialized enzyme, then survive the phage bloom your own success invites.",
    "citation": "Pasulka et al. (2013) 'Interrogating marine virus-host interactions and elemental transfer with BONCAT and nanoSIMS'; De Alteris & Pasulka squid-fall microbial succession studies, Gulf of California.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Gulf of California jumbo squid (Dosidicus gigas) carcass/beak falls and chitinolytic Vibrio/Pseudoalteromonas bloom-and-phage-crash succession"
  },
